        "heading": "Introduction",
        "text": "Porsche Engineering (Porsche Engineering Group GmbH) was established in 2001 as a wholly owned subsidiary of Porsche AG , with headquarters in Weissach , and traces its history back to 1931 when Porsche created its first engineering office subsidiary. Porsche Engineering Group has been re-organized into Porsche Consulting (subsidiary of Porsche AG ) and Porsche Engineering (subsidiary of Porsche SE ).\nPorsche Engineering has offered consultancy services to various other car manufacturers for many years including Audi , Mercedes-Benz , Opel , Studebaker , Lada , SEAT , and Zastava Automobiles . Since 2012 the company has managed the former FIAT owned Nardò Ring in Italy."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Operations",
        "text": "Porsche Engineering develops and tests vehicles, components and software for Porsche and other customers. It is based at Porsche's development centre in Weissach , Germany, with additional teams in Bietigheim-Bissingen, Mönsheim, Leipzig and Wolfsburg.\nThe company opened its Prague office in 2001 and expanded to Ostrava in 2018. Work in Prague includes body and chassis engineering, computer simulation, electronics and software. The Ostrava teams develop software and integrate and test vehicle electronics. Both offices contributed to the Porsche Taycan 's battery-management software, which calculates the battery's remaining charge and monitors its condition.\nPorsche Engineering opened its first Romanian office in Cluj-Napoca in 2016, followed by Timișoara in 2021. Engineers at both locations work on driver-assistance systems, infotainment, connectivity and electric vehicles. Their work also includes artificial intelligence and data processing.\nThe Nardò Technical Center in southern Italy has been part of Porsche Engineering since 2012. The test centre covers 700 hectares and has 20 tracks and testing facilities, including a 12.6-kilometre high-speed ring. A battery testing centre opened in 2021, followed by a software development office in nearby Lecce in 2023.\nThe company's offices in Shanghai and Beijing develop vehicle technologies for the Chinese market, including automated driving and connectivity systems. In November 2025, the Shanghai operation became part of a shared research and development centre with Porsche's local Technical Division and Porsche Digital China."
      },
      {
        "heading": "External links",
        "text": "Official website"
      }
    ],
    "full_text": "Introduction\nPorsche Engineering (Porsche Engineering Group GmbH) was established in 2001 as a wholly owned subsidiary of Porsche AG , with headquarters in Weissach , and traces its history back to 1931 when Porsche created its first engineering office subsidiary. Porsche Engineering Group has been re-organized into Porsche Consulting (subsidiary of Porsche AG ) and Porsche Engineering (subsidiary of Porsche SE ).\nPorsche Engineering has offered consultancy services to various other car manufacturers for many years including Audi , Mercedes-Benz , Opel , Studebaker , Lada , SEAT , and Zastava Automobiles . Since 2012 the company has managed the former FIAT owned Nardò Ring in Italy.\n\nOperations\nPorsche Engineering develops and tests vehicles, components and software for Porsche and other customers. It is based at Porsche's development centre in Weissach , Germany, with additional teams in Bietigheim-Bissingen, Mönsheim, Leipzig and Wolfsburg.\nThe company opened its Prague office in 2001 and expanded to Ostrava in 2018. Work in Prague includes body and chassis engineering, computer simulation, electronics and software. The Ostrava teams develop software and integrate and test vehicle electronics. Both offices contributed to the Porsche Taycan 's battery-management software, which calculates the battery's remaining charge and monitors its condition.\nPorsche Engineering opened its first Romanian office in Cluj-Napoca in 2016, followed by Timișoara in 2021. Engineers at both locations work on driver-assistance systems, infotainment, connectivity and electric vehicles. Their work also includes artificial intelligence and data processing.\nThe Nardò Technical Center in southern Italy has been part of Porsche Engineering since 2012. The test centre covers 700 hectares and has 20 tracks and testing facilities, including a 12.6-kilometre high-speed ring. A battery testing centre opened in 2021, followed by a software development office in nearby Lecce in 2023.\nThe company's offices in Shanghai and Beijing develop vehicle technologies for the Chinese market, including automated driving and connectivity systems. In November 2025, the Shanghai operation became part of a shared research and development centre with Porsche's local Technical Division and Porsche Digital China.\n\nExternal links\nOfficial website"
  },
